# Line endings and binary-ness, decided by git rather than by whichever editor
# or platform last touched a file. The companion to `.editorconfig`: that one
# tells an editor what to write, and this one makes git normalize what it
# stores, which is the half an editor's settings cannot reach.
#
# Why it earns its place: this package is developed on Linux and GhostBSD and
# its live checks are run from a Windows box, where a global `core.autocrlf`
# (neither repository pins one) would otherwise check these files out with
# CRLF and hand back a whole-file diff on the next commit from anywhere else.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` is what it puts in the
# working tree even on Windows. Every tracked file here already has LF in the
# index -- measured, not assumed -- so there is nothing to renormalize for this
# to take effect; it takes effect on the way in from here on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text at all: git must never translate these, and showing a diff of one is
# meaningless. The images are the demo GIF and the captured screenshots under
# examples/artifacts/.
*.gif binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary

# Build and cache artifacts, named so that a stray one is classified by this
# file rather than by whatever bytes it happens to contain.
*.pyc binary
*.whl binary
*.zip binary
*.tar.gz binary
